WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.920
Linux forensics depends on understanding system layout, user artifacts, logs, services, files, and network activity.

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:09.620
The filesystem hierarchy gives analysts a map.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.920
User and account artifacts help identify who could access the system.

00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:16.460
Logs and journals provide event history.

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:21.800
Services, scheduled tasks, and startup files help explain what runs and why.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.020
File metadata and network records add more pieces to the timeline.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.600
Strong analysis preserves evidence and correlates sources.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:40.540
A shell history entry, SSH login, changed service file, modified binary, web request, or suspicious timestamp may be important, but each artifact needs context.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:46.520
Analysts should compare records, look for consistency, identify gaps, and document uncertainty.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:51.780
One artifact can be incomplete, misleading, altered, or misunderstood.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.920
Collection and preservation shape the quality of the investigation.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:00.540
Live systems change as they are examined, and some evidence disappears quickly.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:11.880
Disk images, snapshots, targeted collections, memory captures, log exports, hashes, chain of custody records, and case notes all help make the work more defensible.

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:15.300
The right approach depends on the incident and the system.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.760
The goal is defensible investigation, not guessing from isolated artifacts.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:25.260
Linux forensic work should be authorized, careful, evidence-focused, and honest about limitations.

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:36.200
When analysts preserve context, build timelines, and communicate findings clearly, they help incident responders, system owners, legal teams, and leadership make better decisions.
